<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carolingian Renaissance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carolingian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> was a revival of learning, literature and the arts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> in the late eighth and ninth centuries. Led by scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, it promoted education, church reform and the copying of classical and Christian texts. Many ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works survive because they were preserved in this period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance.jpg" alt="Carolingian Renaissance" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Library · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minuscule_caroline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Carolingian Renaissance</b> — the programme of educational and cultural renewal associated with the palace school at Aachen and with scholars such as Alcuin of York, Theodulf of Orléans, Paul the Deacon and Einhard — spread the Carolingian minuscule script that preserved classical texts and eased administrative correspondence,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Isidore of Seville became one of the most quoted scholars of the Middle Ages, and Theodulf of Orléans, fleeing to the Franks, joined the <b>Carolingian Renaissance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
